From 5fd6ce3d053f9477681be81a1be08cb3f5958523 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Piotr Wilkin <ilintar@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 23:44:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] llama : gather lazy tensor rows with direct reads

Port of ggml-org/llama.cpp#28136. A lazily read table (TENSOR_READ_LAZY)
currently hands its rows to ggml_get_rows through the mmap, so every row
costs a demand fault: a whole page read to reach the ~90 bytes of a PLE
row, one fault at a time, and the pages then accumulate in the page cache.

The row indices of an entire ubatch are known host-side before the graph
runs, so they can instead be deduplicated, sorted into file order and read
in parallel. --lazy-mode on-direct does that, and llama_lazy_reader hands
out F32 rows exactly as ggml_get_rows would.

The original places the reader, a staged input tensor, a staging buffer and
an if/else in each arch that wants it, which is what it was asked not to do.
Here an arch says nothing about direct reads at all: every tensor the loader
reads lazily gets a reader keyed by the tensor, and llm_graph_lazy_rows is
the one place that decides between a ggml_get_rows node and rows staged in
set_input(). An arch swaps its ggml_get_rows for the helper's build() and
forwards set_rows()/can_reuse() - qwen4exp and gemma4 both shrink.

llama_file grows a positional read (pread, or ReadFile with an OVERLAPPED
offset), so no part of this is conditional on the platform. The reader holds
one buffered file per worker: read_at() is not thread-safe everywhere, and
the loader's own descriptor may carry direct I/O, whose alignment rules a
single row read cannot meet.

qwen4exp, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next IQ4_NL, gfx1151, 27 GiB PLE table:

  pp2048   277.36 -> 484.12 t/s   +75%
  pp8192   404.91 -> 450.67 t/s   +11%
  tg128     24.02 ->  24.24 t/s    flat

The win shrinks with prompt length as compute takes over, and with a warm
page cache, since a warm mmap fault is minor. Greedy output is identical
byte for byte to --lazy-mode on and off.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5
